t: say whether any control cell like `<linkedin>` or `<metadata>` kills it); **M21** put back main's `/<script[^>]*>.*?<\/script>/gi` pair pattern in place of `:226` (predict the reject cells red); **M22** delete main's own `/javascript:/gi` line (`:227`) and **M23** `/vbscript:/gi` (`:228`) (informational: they are main's, not the PR's; say whether a cell pins them); an UNMUTATED copy (control, GREEN 39/39). Survivors are classified one by one: EQUIVALENT (proved), TEST GAP (the code is right at the head but no cell pins that behaviour), or DEFECT (the head's behaviour is wrong). Grading: a TEST GAP on a ruled-in input of the aim (a scheme in the three-scheme list, any opening tag of the six, case, space, nesting) = Major (the cells are this PR's proof, and the test file is byte-equal to #67's so a new cell is a new round); a TEST GAP on an edge beyond the aim (order of removals on split input) = Minor; a DEFECT = Blocker.
**(b) Bypass hunt, by node probe of the head module** (`require` `backend/validators.js` in `worktrees/b647-merge`, calling `sanitizeInput(x, false)`, `sanitizeInput(x, true)`, `validateXSSInput(x)` and the whole `validateInput` middleware with a hand-made `req`/`res`; no server). Corpus (print every input, output and verdict; each family has at least one control that must be caught): case (`JaVaScRiPt:`); whitespace inside the colon gap (`data \t:`) and INSIDE the scheme word (`java\tscript:`, `java\nscript:`, `java\rscript:`, a form URL parsers strip) ; a control byte before, inside and after the scheme (`\x01javascript:`, `java\u0000script:`, `javascript\u0000:`, `<\u0000script`); percent-encoded (`%6Aavascript:`, `javascript%3A`); entity-encoded (`&#106;avascript:`, `&#x6A;avascript:`, `javascript&colon;`, `javascript&#58;`); backslash/unicode-escape text (`javascript:`); Unicode look-alikes and zero-width characters (fullwidth colon, U+200B or U+00AD inside the word, a dotless or long-s letter); nested and split schemes in BOTH modes (`javajavascript:script:`, `dadata:ta:`, `vbvbscript:script:`, three levels deep) and schemes split by characters the GENERAL branch removes (`java<>script:`, `java'script:`, `java;script:`, `java$script:`, `java&script:`); opening-tag forms (`<script`, `<SCRIPT`, `<` + newline + `script`, `< script`, `<script/x>`, `<!--<script`, `<scr<script>ipt>`, `<scr\u0000ipt`, `</script >`, `<iframe`, `<object`, `<embed`, `<link`, `<meta`, and the near misses `<scripts`, `<linkedin>`, `<metadata>`); other elements and handlers the filter never named (`<img src=x onerror=...>`, `<svg onload=...>`, `<body onfocus=...>`, `<a href=...>`, `<style>`, `<base>`, `<form>`, `<math>`, handlers `onfocus=`, `onblur=`, `ontoggle=` against `validateXSSInput`, which tests four). **Cross-stage assembly (a candidate bypass YOU must run, because `validateInput` tests the ORIGINAL value after sanitising into `obj[key]`):** (i) KQL field (`kqlQuery` etc.): `<scr` + `data:` + `ipt src=x` is stripped by the sanitiser into `<script src=x` while `validateXSSInput` saw the unstripped original and approved it; (ii) general field: `onlo` + `data:` + `ad=` leaves `onload=` AFTER the handler `replace` ran; (iii) the scheme assembled from pieces across the removed-character set. For each, run the middleware end to end and print what lands in `req.body` and whether a 400 was sent. **Classify EVERY candidate that survives the sanitiser and is accepted by the validator** into: (A) EXECUTABLE IN CONTEXT (the output reaches an HTML/attribute/URL sink by your READING of `backend/server.js` and `static/**` or `public/**`: grep `innerHTML`, `res.send(`, `res.type('html')`, `href=`, template strings that interpolate stored fields; name the sink, `path:line`); (B) NOT REACHABLE (stored and returned as JSON, or used as a KQL/SQL text; name how); (C) OUT OF THE RULED SCOPE (the reject filter's element and handler lists are the old, unchanged lists; encodings and escape-on-output were ruled out of this round). Grading: an (A) bypass INSIDE the aim (the three schemes or the six opening tags, however spelled) that reaches a sink = Blocker (state the sink); an (A) bypass outside the aim = Major only if a CodeQL rule of the PR's own alerts would also fire, otherwise Minor; (B) = Note with the proof of non-reachability; (C) = Note listed in FOR TUESDAY for the product owner (a follow-up ticket, not this PR). Never exploit anything; probes are string-in string-out on the module.
**(c) ReDoS and cost, timed:** inputs (print the length and the median of 3 `process.hrtime` timings for `sanitizeInput` in both modes and `validateXSSInput`): `data` + n spaces (no colon); `data ` x n; `vbscript` x n; `<` + n spaces; `< ` x n; `<\n` x n; `a:` x n; `d` x n + `ata:`; **the fixed-point worst case, nesting depth k: `java` x k + `script:` x k (each pass peels one layer, so passes = k and the cost is quadratic in k: measure it)**; the same input at main's `validators.js` and the old `<script` x n against main's pair pattern for comparison. n doubles from 10,000 to the realistic limit: the JSON body limit read at the head (`express.json` `limit: '5mb'`, `server.js`; `urlencoded` 50mb: say which routes `validateInput` is mounted on, whether before or after authentication, and the largest string a request can carry). Report the doubling ratio (2x = linear, 4x = quadratic) and the time at the limit. Grading: any shape taking more than 1 s at the body limit on an unauthenticated route = Major; quadratic but under 1 s at the limit = Note with the figure; linear = pass. A catastrophic (exponential) shape = Blocker. Say that CodeQL `js/redos` and `js/polynomial-redos` read 0 only if you read them (Check 5); otherwise UNMEASURED.
